/**

@page page_compiler_specific_settings_home Vendor Software Stacks and System- and Compiler-specific Settings

\li @subpage tarch_accelerator_SYCL

\tableofcontents

Peano keeps system- and compiler-specific settings in two places:

- The build configuration is driven entirely by CMake. Optional features such
  as GPU backends and I/O libraries are selected through `PEANO_WITH_*`
  options, and the resulting preprocessor defines and compiler
  and linker flags are attached to the `PeanoOptions` interface target, so
  every library and application built on top of Peano inherits them. CMake
  also writes a `PeanoConfig.cmake` file into the build directory; the Python
  API reads this file to learn about the configuration and to generate
  consistent makefiles for the codes built on top of Peano.
- At compile time, a single header derives a small set of portability macros
  from the compiler that is actually in use (through standard predefined
  preprocessor symbols such as `__NVCOMPILER`, `__GNUC__` or `__clang__`).
  This header hides the differences between the compilers from the rest of the
  code.

The page below discusses how to build Peano with the major vendor toolchains
and provides some hints on compiler and linker flags for each of them.
Before the particular toolchains, a general remark on how the compiler
dependencies are managed:


# Realisation of Peano's internal compiler-specific switches

Peano relies on a single header tarch/CompilerSpecificSettings.h.
This header inspects the predefined macros of the compiler in use and, on this
basis, defines a small set of portability macros (alignment hints, inlining
attributes, SIMD and unrolling pragmas, and switches for compilers that do not
support certain OpenMP constructs).

Whenever we find incompatibilities between different compilers, we try to
resolve them through defines within this header.
This way, we avoid that some "fixes" are spread over the whole code.
Most expressions within the compiler-specific settings header can manually
be overwritten via defines.
Consult the file itself for details.

The build defines provided by CMake (for example `PEANO_WITH_ALIGNMENT`
or `PEANO_WITH_SIMDLEN`) feed into this header. Whenever
certain macro combinations have knock-on effects on other features, they are
evaluated here and mapped onto further portability macros.

There are exceptions to this rule: If you have a certain GPU backend, you
might have to annotate functions in a certain way. Such information is not
covered within CompilerSpecificSettings (it has nothing to do with a particular
compiler choice), but is found directly within the headers of the respective
namespace in the technical architecture.


# Vendor toolchains and compiler settings

All builds use CMake. The recipes below set the compiler via the standard
`-DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER` / `-DCMAKE_C_COMPILER` switches, toggle features through
`PEANO_WITH_*` options, and pass any vendor-specific compiler or linker flags
via the semicolon-separated lists `PEANO_EXTRA_CXX_FLAGS` and
`PEANO_EXTRA_LINK_FLAGS`. The C++ standard is fixed to C++20. The general
shape of a configure call is

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
mkdir build && cd build
cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=... [options]
make -j
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Most GPU backends derive the actual offloading flags themselves from the chosen
backend (`PEANO_WITH_GPU_BACKEND`) and the target architecture
(`PEANO_WITH_GPU_ARCH`, e.g. `sm_90`, `gfx90a` or `spir64`), so for those you
usually do not have to spell out the offloading flags by hand.

## GNU

### OpenMP and OpenMP offloading

GCC offers OpenMP host threading out of the box and OpenMP GPU offloading via
`PEANO_WITH_GPU_BACKEND=ompt`. The OMPT backend selects the matching
`-foffload=...` flag for the requested architecture automatically.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
  -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=gcc -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=g++ \
  -DPEANO_WITH_GPU_BACKEND=ompt -DPEANO_WITH_GPU_ARCH=sm_90 \
  -DPEANO_EXTRA_CXX_FLAGS="-march=native"
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Drop the two GPU options (and the architecture) for a pure host build.


## Intel (oneAPI)

<img width="10%" src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/f/fa/OneAPI-rgb-3000.png" />

The Intel oneAPI compilers are `icx` (C) and `icpx` (C++). Select them through
the standard CMake compiler switches.

### Build modes and tracing

Trace and annotation data only become available if you do not build in release
mode. Switch to the trace mode (or assertions or debug) - compare the general
remarks on @ref page_architecture_home "Peano's build variants" - to get traces
or annotation information. For further discussion on logging, please see the
\ref tarch_logging "generic logging description for Peano".

If you analyse threaded runs with the Intel tools, the following flags are
useful raw compiler hints. They are passed via `PEANO_EXTRA_CXX_FLAGS`:

- `-DTBB_USE_THREADING_TOOLS -parallel-source-info=2` for all builds, and
- additionally `-DTBB_USE_ASSERT` for debug and assert builds.


### OpenMP and OpenMP offloading

For Intel GPUs use the OMPT backend with the `spir64` target. The backend adds
`-fopenmp-targets=spir64` for you; `-fiopenmp` is Intel's recommended OpenMP
front-end flag for performance and features.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
  -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=icx -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=icpx \
  -DPEANO_WITH_GPU_BACKEND=ompt -DPEANO_WITH_GPU_ARCH=spir64 \
  -DPEANO_EXTRA_CXX_FLAGS="-fiopenmp;-march=native"
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


### SYCL and SYCL offloading

SYCL is directly supported via `icpx`. Select it as the C++ compiler and pick a
SYCL-capable GPU backend (`native` with an Intel `spir64` architecture, or
`acpp`). The build adds `-fsycl -fsycl-targets=spir64` for you, which makes all
SYCL headers known to `icpx` and tells the linker to add the SYCL libraries and
to embed both the device and the host kernels into the executable.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
  -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=icx -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=icpx \
  -DPEANO_WITH_GPU_BACKEND=native -DPEANO_WITH_GPU_ARCH=spir64
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

If you add `-lsycl` alone and leave the linking to the plain linker, the build
succeeds, but the very first time you invoke a SYCL kernel you get a message
similar to

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
terminate called after throwing an instance of 'sycl::_V1::runtime_error'
  what():  No kernel named <mangled SYCL kernel symbol> was found -46 (PI_ERROR_INVALID_KERNEL_NAME)
Aborted (core dumped)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

To offload SYCL onto NVIDIA or AMD GPUs through the oneAPI plugins
(Intel LLVM, Codeplay), set `PEANO_WITH_GPU_ARCH` to the corresponding
`sm_XX` (NVIDIA) or `gfxXXX` (AMD) architecture and add the matching SYCL
target as a raw compiler and linker hint, for example via

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
-DPEANO_EXTRA_CXX_FLAGS="-fsycl-targets=nvptx64-nvidia-cuda;-fp-model=consistent"
-DPEANO_EXTRA_LINK_FLAGS="-fsycl-targets=nvptx64-nvidia-cuda"
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

(or `-fsycl-targets=amdgcn-amd-amdhsa` for AMD), where the architecture itself
is supplied through `PEANO_WITH_GPU_ARCH`.

### MPI

Intel's MPI compiler wrapper is `mpiicpc`, and oneAPI expects `icpx` as its C++
compiler. To make the wrapper call `icpx`, export the corresponding environment
variable and point the CMake compiler at the wrapper:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
export I_MPI_CXX=icpx
cmake .. -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=mpiicpc
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


## LLVM

### OpenMP

Clang offers OpenMP GPU offloading through the OMPT backend. The backend
derives the `-fopenmp-targets=nvptx64-nvidia-cuda --offload-arch=...` flags from
the requested architecture, so you only have to name the backend and the GPU
architecture.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
  -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=clang++ \
  -DPEANO_WITH_GPU_BACKEND=ompt -DPEANO_WITH_GPU_ARCH=sm_90 \
  -DPEANO_EXTRA_CXX_FLAGS="-march=native"
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Replace `sm_90` by the actual compute capability of the device.


## NVIDIA NVC++ (NVHPC)

<img width="20%" src="https://www.nvidia.com/content/dam/en-zz/Solutions/about-nvidia/logo-and-brand/01-nvidia-logo-vert-500x200-2c50-p@2x.png" />

The NVIDIA HPC compiler is `nvc++`. As `nvc++` is also used as the C compiler
in this toolchain, point both `CMAKE_C_COMPILER` and `CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER` at it.


### OpenMP and OpenMP offloading

NVIDIA's compiler supports a subset of recent OpenMP task features. CMake
tests whether iterator expressions are accepted in task dependencies; when
the probe fails, the tasking layer uses conservative dependency waits that
preserve ordering with less parallelism.

The OMPT backend translates the requested architecture into the `-mp=gpu
-gpu=ccXX` flags that NVHPC expects:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
  -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=nvc++ -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=nvc++ \
  -DPEANO_WITH_GPU_BACKEND=ompt -DPEANO_WITH_GPU_ARCH=sm_90 \
  -DPEANO_EXTRA_CXX_FLAGS="--diag_suppress=unrecognized_attribute;-Munroll=c:100;-march=native"
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Replace `sm_90` by the actual compute capability of the device. To experiment
with C++ standard parallelism offloading you can add `-stdpar=gpu` to
`PEANO_EXTRA_CXX_FLAGS`.


## AMD

For the AMD toolchain you need to load either ROCm or AOMP. Both modules ship
AMD's modified version of Clang, `amdclang`/`amdclang++`. Point both CMake
compiler variables at it.

### OpenMP and OpenMP offloading

The OMPT backend emits the AMD offloading flags
(`-fopenmp-targets=amdgcn-amd-amdhsa --offload-arch=gfxXXX`) for the requested
architecture:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
  -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=amdclang -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=amdclang++ \
  -DPEANO_WITH_GPU_BACKEND=ompt -DPEANO_WITH_GPU_ARCH=gfx90a \
  -DPEANO_EXTRA_CXX_FLAGS="-march=native"
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Replace `gfx90a` by the actual architecture of the device.

*/
